---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Resources — Primary Sources and Study Tools | devsyllabus"
  description="Curated resources for computer science study: standards (RFCs), official documentation, canonical textbooks, and how to use them."
  path="/resources/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Resources' }]} />

  <div class="doc">
  <h1>Resources</h1>
  <p>
    The sources our notes cite, plus tools worth knowing. These are
    recommendations only — see our
    <a href="/sources/">source citation policy</a> and
    <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" rel="noopener">RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics</a>
      — the authoritative definition of methods, headers, and status codes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/" rel="noopener">RFC Editor</a>
      — the full RFC index, searchable.
    </li>
    <li>
      ISO/IEC 9075 — the SQL standard. The editions published by ISO are
      the definition of the language; most courses teach a close subset.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Official documentation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP" rel="noopener">MDN HTTP reference</a>
      — practical, accurate HTTP documentation, with good coverage of
      caching and conditional requests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript" rel="noopener">MDN JavaScript reference</a>
      — the same standard of documentation for the language itself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Canonical textbooks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <em>The C Programming Language</em> (Kernighan and Ritchie) — still
      the reference for C semantics and idiom.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Database System Concepts</em> (Silberschatz, Korth, and Sudarshan)
      — the standard relational-model and normalization reference.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>An Introduction to Database Systems</em> (C. J. Date) — deeper
      treatment of relational theory, useful for theory-heavy courses.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Foundational reading</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm" rel="noopener">
        Fielding's 2000 dissertation, chapter 5
      </a>
      — the original definition of REST. Reading it resolves most
      "is this RESTful" debates.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use these</h2>
  <p>
    Study the devsyllabus notes first, then verify against the primary
    source when a detail matters. That habit is what our
    <a href="/blog/active-recall-spaced-repetition/">study technique article</a>
    recommends for exam-prep anyway: retrieval first, reference second.
  </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
